## Physical 0214 / printed 207

Top starts with the continuation fragment "在数轴上的位置。" There is no
new numbered unit heading on this page.
Definition 2: the difference between x and its integer part is x's fractional
part. The mathematical symbol is braces {x}, not ordinary parentheses.
Formula: {x}=x-[x].
The examples, including the explanatory prose, say:
{6.4}=6.4-[6.4]=6.4-6=0.4 (6.4's fractional part equals 0.4);
{11}=11-[11]=11-11=0 (11's fractional part equals 0);
{-3.8}=-3.8-[-3.8]=-3.8-(-4)=0.2
(-3.8's fractional part equals 0.2).
The first and third explanatory parentheticals wrap to a second print line.

Inequalities:
[x] <= x < [x]+1, hence 0 <= x-[x] < 1 and 0 <= {x} < 1.
The non-strict comparisons have the slanted double-line less-or-equal glyph.
Prose explicitly states the fractional part is a nonnegative number less than 1.
The definition gives {x}=x-[x], therefore x=[x]+{x}; every number is the
sum of its integer and fractional parts. The x=[x]+{x} expression wraps after +.
Further examples, two print rows and two columns:
25.8=25+0.8; -3.7=-4+0.3;
0.12=0+0.12; -0.476=-1+0.524.

The function y=[x] has domain the real numbers; its graph is Figure 79.
Examples: 0 <= x < 1 gives y=0; 1 <= x < 2 gives y=1;
-1 <= x < 0 gives y=-1 (the last inequality wraps before <0).
Generally n <= x < n+1, n integer, gives y=n.
The interval printed is [n,n+1[, not a closed right bracket or parentheses.
Within each such interval the value equals n.
No exercises or figure pixels on this page. Footer: 207.

